# How lines end.
#
# Every text file ends each line with a hidden character meaning "new line".
# Windows writes two of them, everything else writes one. Nobody ever sees
# either, but git compares files character by character, so the same level
# saved on two machines reads as though every single line changed. That is
# what a whole-file conflict over nothing actually is.
#
# text=auto settles it: git keeps ONE style in the repository and hands each
# machine the style it expects on the way out. The editor can go on writing
# Windows endings and nothing downstream notices.
* text=auto

# Batch files are read by the Windows command processor, which wants CRLF
# wherever the file came from.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Art, sound and fonts are NOT text. Without these lines git decides for
# itself, and a picture that happens to look textual enough would have its
# line endings "corrected" — which is to say, corrupted. Say so explicitly.
*.png binary
*.bmp binary
*.ico binary
*.wav binary
*.ttf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.xnb binary
